[{:seon.task/name "a-test-runs-in-the-world"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: \"we need a system for our own agents and it's simpler to say this is a base world you can get (I suppose a custom one should be allowed if for good reason) and customize and then it'll run fast and we can optimize for one happy path\"; \"I think my.test is better\" (the API is the agent's, beside my.task and my.agent). And: \"Shrink the tests down to sizes that capture the error class they are looking for ... seon agents [should] have tests that run over a configured time ... rejected\".
Built: the world and its API, my.test (src/my/test.clj: world, with, code, page, task, without, cost; docs/testing.md#testing-in-the-world; tests test/my/test_test.clj: my.test-test/the-world-is-built-once-per-process, a-fixture-adds-to-the-world-and-leaves-it-as-it-was, a-world-test-runs-far-under-the-ceiling, the-pages-example-runs). A test over it costs about a millisecond and no store.
What remains. (1) The tests that import the real program still run over the ceiling: when measured (2026-10-30), 90 of 1,022 in-process tests ran over 2e8 ticks, 236 s of the in-process suite's 354 s, the top 18 over 1e9 (seon.vector-test/the-pyramid-ranks-as-an-exact-search 8 G, seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current 5.3 G, the whole-src imports of seon.source-test and the whole-program health of seon.problems-test), and every src change reran the whole-program ones. Re-read the verdicts for today's list, then move those onto the world (each keeps its claim; a cost proof keeps the size where its growth shows and drops the rest; a test that scanned all of src or the wiki takes a fixture; the context session reviews the diff of the ones in problems, render, render-cost, context, context-cost and ctx). 30 files still build on seon.test-store/test-db and test-branch! (src and test). (2) :seon.config.agent/test-ticks is still 1e10 (resources/seon/config/default.edn); it drops to 2e8, a test past it red, its error naming my.test/world and the shrink. (3) Deletes: seon.db/seed-tx's example program (18 namespaces of 20 one-line functions, still the test store's seed) and seon.test-store/test-db and test-branch! once their callers use my.test (or they become the two my.test calls)."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.config-test/the-test-ceiling-is-a-second-of-work}
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-agent-is-made-one-way"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: flow. The owner, 2026-11-16: we rewrite or delete bad tests, never conform to them. CAUSE: about 20 test harnesses each fake an agent by hand: reset! seon.agent/state with its fields, the process's start fields among them, restored in a finally. G2 (seon.agent/*start*) broke every copy, and its fix forward (jolt/flow 51fb4adec1) patched each to bind *start*, which conforms to the design instead of removing it. PRIOR WORK: my.test/eval-in (src/my/test.clj) is the one mechanism already: a test agent's start bound, its state set and restored; ctx-test's enter-agent!/end-agent! is a second (context folds it into a with-agent form); the copies are agent_test's work-with, delivery-world's as, the merge harnesses (8, each a store connection, a socketpair and a hand-made state), the fold test, and the harnesses in repl_test, schema_test, vector_test, ctx/wire_test and program_cost_test (whose helper never restores the state). Also 39 eval strings read process internals ((:key @seon.agent/state) and the like): a form in a string is no reach edge, so a change to that state reran none of them. PLAN: one form, beside eval-in in my.test, that runs a body as a test agent from one map (its start fields bound, the rest its state, both restored), the copies replaced by it and deleted; a test reads an agent's identity through the agent's API, never its internals. COST: per test one binding and two resets, O(fields); no store unless the test names one. PROOFS: the 58 tests the G2 fix forward reaches stay green; a grep for (reset! seon.agent/state in test finds only the one form."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}]
